      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "Mouraria gave birth to fado when Maria Severa first sang in its streets, taking the music of the people to the aristocracy.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        21
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.lisbonportugaltourism.com/guide/mouraria.html",
      "category": "culture",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "Christopher’s Church, built in the 13th century, was rebuilt after a 16th‑century fire and still stands in Mouraria’s historic core.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        23
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.lisbonportugaltourism.com/guide/mouraria.html",
      "category": "landmark",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "A series of steps in Mouraria leads to a mural that pays tribute to the city’s legendary fado singers.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        22
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.lisbonportugaltourism.com/guide/mouraria.html",
      "category": "culture",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "Mouraria, like Alfama and Bairro Alto, is one of Lisbon’s historic quarters that share a distinctive culture and architectural landmarks.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        5
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
